### What is the feature you are proposing?

The [`validator`](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/16a9a949c66bd561e55fffba1580ddc18ef30857/src/validator/validator.ts#L30) function is used by most of the hono middleware packages (e.g. [`@hono/zod-validator`](https://github.com/honojs/middleware/tree/main/packages/zod-validator)) to validate incoming request data, automatically throw errors where applicable, and provided typed data back via `c.req.valid(...)`. The current implementation will treat all middleware calls as intersecting and ensure that ALL the data validates currently before passing the request through to the route handler:

```ts
app.post(
  '/',
  zValidator('query', z.object({ q: z.string().optional() })),
  zValidator('json', z.object({ name: z.string() })),
  async (c) => {
    // Both typed correctly
    const query = c.req.valid('query');
    const json = c.req.valid('json');
  }
);
```

However, requiring ALL the validators to be used on every request fails in the case of `json` and `form` data which cannot exist simultaneously because they are both part of the `body` of the request. (See https://github.com/honojs/middleware/issues/907 for some context). Requests will always fail if a route tries to validate both `json` and `form` data simultaneously. 

The more appropriate expectation for `body` data in this case would be to validate either `json` OR `form` data based on the incoming content type. This is a valid way to discriminate incoming data, and it would be great for the native hono validation to support it.

-------

What I propose is to add support for `body` inside of [`ValidationTargets`](https://github.com/honojs/hono/blob/16a9a949c66bd561e55fffba1580ddc18ef30857/src/types.ts#L1930) that would take in a dictionary of appropriate targets along with a validation function for each. When validating the incoming requests, it would only validate the incoming data against the function matching the incoming content type:

```ts
app.post(
  '/',
  validator('body', {
    json: (value, c) => {
      // Only called when Content-Type = 'application/json'
    },
    form: (value, c) => {
      // Only called when Content-Type = 'application/form-data'
    }
  }),
  async (c) => {
    if (c.req.header('User-Agent') === 'application/json') {
      const json = c.req.valid('json');
    } else {
     const data = c.req.valid('form');
    }
  }
);
```

